    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The brain integrates internal and external cues to initiate reproduction. To study this integration, we used a well-studied model organism, an African cichlid fish, Astatotilapia burtoni. When female A. burtoni ovulate, a rise in the hormone prostaglandin F2α (PGF2α) signals to the brain that she is ready to spawn.  If a male courts her and stimulates her through visual, auditory and olfactory cues, then spawning will occur.  However, the mechanisms and location of external and internal signal integration is not well understood.  In mammals and goldfish, PGF2α is known to regulate reproductive behavior, and it has been shown to be sufficient to induce spawning in female A. burtoni.  My research asked whether PGF2α is necessary for spawning in A. burtoni.  To test this hypothesis, we mutated the gene for the PGF2α receptor (Ptgfr), using clustered, regularly interspaced, short palindromic repeats (CRISPR). Fish carrying mutations in this gene failed to spawn during 30-minute assays, while all the control females spawned.  These experiments show that PGF2α is necessary for spawning in female A. burtoni, setting the groundwork for identifying brain regions and neurons important for integrating internal and external signals.  Further studies will determine how this hormone works in conjunction with external signals in the A. burtoni brain to regulate reproduction.  These experiments could be broadly informative across vertebrate species since PGF2α plays a conserved role in vertebrate reproduction.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
